\section{Proportional bias diagnostic}{

The proportional bias panel of \code{check()} plots the differences (centred)
against the means, on the analysis scale (the log scale for
log-transformed data), whether or not the fit used \code{prop_bias}. Because
cov(x - y, x + y) = var(x) - var(y), a slope of the differences on the
means only shows that the two methods have unequal total variance; the
test of the slope is the Pitman-Morgan test of equal variances. Unequal
variances arise from a true proportional bias (y = a + b x with b not 1),
but also from unequal measurement error or from a prior calibration of one
method on the other (Bland & Altman, 1999; Hopkins, 2004). With one
measurement per method these cannot be told apart, and \code{check()} says so
when the test is significant.

What is tested depends on the design:
\itemize{
\item \strong{One measurement per method} (\code{data_type = "simple"}): the
Pitman-Morgan test (t on n - 2 df). With \code{error.ratio}, the Deming
slope of y on x for that error ratio is tested against 1 instead (the
test reported by \code{\link[=dem_reg]{dem_reg()}}). The error variances implied by the
Deming fit give the slope expected from unequal error.
\item \strong{Replicates} (\code{data_type = "reps"}): the test uses subject means
and is corrected for unequal measurement error. The covariance of the
subject-mean differences and sums is compared with its expected value
without proportional bias, \eqn{s^2_{wx}\overline{1/m_x} -
    s^2_{wy}\overline{1/m_y}}, from the pooled within-subject variances,
rather than with zero. The standard error is a jackknife over
subjects (re-estimating the within-subject variances) with n - 1 df.
Only the difference of the error variances enters, so an occasion
effect shared by both methods (linked replicates) cancels. Complete
pairs of measurements are used.
\item \strong{Nested data} (\code{data_type = "nest"}): the true value changes
within a subject, so the error variances cannot be estimated; the slope
is tested with a random-intercept model (likelihood ratio test) and is
subject to the same limitation as one measurement per method.
}
}

\section{Choosing \code{bias_line}}{

When the test is corrected for measurement error (replicates or
\code{error.ratio}), the panel shows the observed least-squares slope (solid,
with its confidence band, which is not corrected) and a second, dashed
line chosen by \code{bias_line}. The test and its p-value are the same for both
choices; only the picture differs.
\itemize{
\item \strong{"adjusted"} (default): the error-adjusted slope, the slope of the
differences on the means that would be seen without measurement error,
2(1 - beta)/(1 + beta) for y = a + beta x on the true values. Without
proportional bias it is horizontal.
\itemize{
\item Pros: one simple rule (is the dashed line flat?); its slope gives
\code{beta}, the size of the proportional bias, which is reported in the
caption; it applies in the same way to replicates and \code{error.ratio}.
\item Cons: the test is of the covariance, not of this slope, so how far
the line is from flat is not a direct picture of the test statistic;
when the error variances are similar it lies on top of the observed
line and is hard to see.
}
\item \strong{"expected"}: the slope expected from unequal measurement error
alone, with no proportional bias. Without proportional bias the
observed (solid) line should follow it.
\itemize{
\item Pros: the gap between the two lines is what is tested, so the
picture matches the p-value; it shows directly how much of the
observed slope unequal error can explain.
\item Cons: "the solid line should follow the dashed one" is harder to
judge than "the dashed line should be flat"; the size of the
proportional bias (\code{beta}) is not shown (it is in the \code{"prop_bias"}
attribute).
}
}

Neither line shows its own uncertainty; use the p-value (and, for the
size, \code{beta}) rather than judging the lines by eye, particularly with few
subjects.
}

\section{Quadrant and polar plots}{

With repeated measurements of a value that changes within subjects
("trending"), \code{type = "quadrant"} and \code{type = "polar"} show whether the
two methods agree on the \emph{changes} between successive occasions within a
subject. Occasions are ordered by row order within \code{id} for
\code{\link[=agreement_limit]{agreement_limit()}} (\code{data_type = "nest"}), by \code{time} (else row order)
within \code{id} for \code{\link[=tolerance_limit]{tolerance_limit()}}, and by the replicate for
\code{\link[=method_vc]{method_vc()}} with linked replicates; only successive occasions with both
methods measured are used.
\itemize{
\item \strong{Quadrant:} the change by \code{x} against the change by \code{y}, with the line
of identity (dashed) and the exclusion zone (dotted). The concordance
rate in the caption is the share of included changes in which both
methods change in the same direction.
\item \strong{Polar} (Critchley et al., 2011): each change is drawn at a distance
equal to its mean change and at the angle between the two methods'
changes and the line of identity (positive when \code{y} changed more);
changes with a negative mean are turned through 180 degrees. The
angular bias is the mean angle and the radial limits of agreement are
the bias \eqn{\pm z} SD of the angles (with the agreement or prediction
level of the fit).
}

With \code{expected = TRUE}, the caption adds the concordance expected from
the model, the average over the included changes of
\eqn{\Phi(|\Delta| / (\sqrt{2} \sigma_w))}, where \eqn{\Delta} is the
mean change and \eqn{\sigma_w} the within-subject SD of the differences
(Enevoldsen et al., 2026): from the model for \code{\link[=method_vc]{method_vc()}} (the residual
SDs of the pair) and for \code{\link[=tolerance_limit]{tolerance_limit()}} fits with a single
\code{SD.within}, otherwise the pooled within-subject SD of the differences.

Read these plots with care. The concordance rate and the angles depend on
the exclusion zone and on the size of the changes in the data; the polar
exclusion by mean change drops pairs that change in opposite directions;
common thresholds (such as a concordance rate of 92\% or radial limits of
\eqn{\pm 30} degrees) have no statistical basis; there are no confidence
intervals (changes within a subject are correlated, so any would need to
resample subjects); and, like the within-subject limits of agreement,
both plots can miss a method that does not follow changes when the
changes in the data are small. For \code{\link[=method_vc]{method_vc()}} fits, the within-item
reliability and the variance components are the model-based summaries.
}

